      "reasoning": "FIRST gives a mostly correct high-level explanation: Onload accelerates socket operations through a user-space stack, while EFVI bypasses the kernel to access the adapter directly. It omits how Onload interposes on standard socket calls and how EFVI uses receive queues, buffers, and events. SECOND explains epoll and hypothetical FPGA offloading instead of the requested technologies.",
      "evidence": "Onload works at the socket layer, while EFVI bypasses the kernel and directly interacts with the network adapter."
    },
    {
