<first_data>
To send a proposal request to a user's WalletConnect session using a Node.js backend server, you'll need to use the WalletConnect library. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. First, install the WalletConnect library in your project:

```bash
npm install --save @walletconnect/web3-provider @walletconnect/modal
```

2. Import the necessary modules in your server file:

```javascript
const WalletConnectProvider = require("@walletconnect/web3-provider").default;
const WalletConnectModal = require("@walletconnect/modal").default;
```

3. Initialize WalletConnectModal:

```javascript
const walletConnectModal = new WalletConnectModal({
  projectId: "your_project_id", // replace with your project id from WalletConnect
  metadata: {
    name: "Your App Name",
    description: "Your App Description",
    url: "https://yourapp.com",
    icons: ["https://yourapp.com/icon.png"],
  },
});
```

4. Create a WalletConnectProvider instance:

```javascript
const provider = new WalletConnectProvider({
  infuraId: "your_infura_id", // replace with your Infura ID
  bridge: "https://bridge.walletconnect.org",
});
```

5. Connect to the user's wallet:

```javascript
async function connect() {
  try {
    await provider.enable();
    console.log("Connected to WalletConnect!");
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to connect to WalletConnect:", error);
  }
}
```

6. Send a proposal request:

```javascript
async function sendProposal() {
  try {
    const session = await provider.sendCustomRequest({
      method: "eth_sendTransaction",
      params: [
        {
          from: "user_address", // replace with the user's address
          to: "contract_address", // replace with the contract address
          value: "0x00",
          gas: "0x76c0",
          gasPrice: "0x9184e72a000",
          data: "0xd46e8dd67c5d32be8d46e8dd67c5d32be8058bb8eb970870f072445675058bb8eb970870f072445675",
        },
      ],
    });
    console.log("Proposal sent:", session);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to send proposal:", error);
  }
}
```

7. Finally, call the `connect` and `sendProposal` functions:

```javascript
(async () => {
  await connect();
  await sendProposal();
})();
```
</first_data>

<second_data>
To send a proposal request to a user's WalletConnect session using a Node.js backend server, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Install WalletConnect SDK:
First, install the WalletConnect SDK in your project. You can do this using npm:

```bash
npm install @walletconnect/web3
```

2. Import WalletConnect:
Import the WalletConnect SDK in your Node.js file:

```javascript
const WalletConnect = require('@walletconnect/web3');
```

3. Create a WalletConnect instance:
Create a new WalletConnect instance with your project's metadata and a QR code session callback:

```javascript
const walletConnect = new WalletConnect({
  bridge: 'https://bridge.walletconnect.org',
  qrcodeModalOptions: {
    background: '#61dafb',
    showQRCodeInModal: true,
    width: 280,
    height: 280,
  },
});
```

4. Connect to the user's wallet:
Call the `connect` method on the WalletConnect instance to initiate the connection process. This will display a QR code for the user to scan with their WalletConnect-enabled wallet:

```javascript
walletConnect.connect().then((error, pair) => {
  if (error) {
    console.error(error);
    return;
  }

  // Connection successful, save the pair object for future use
  console.log('Connected to WalletConnect!', pair);
});
```

5. Send a proposal request:
Once connected, you can send a proposal request to the user's wallet using the `sendCustomRequest` method. This method allows you to send custom JSON-RPC requests to the connected wallet:

```javascript
const proposal = {
  id: 1,
  jsonrpc: '2.0',
  method: 'eth_sendTransaction',
  params: [
    {
      // Your transaction data here
    },
  ],
};

walletConnect.sendCustomRequest(proposal).then((result) => {
  console.log('Proposal result:', result);
}).catch((error) => {
  console.error('Proposal error:', error);
});
```

6. Handle disconnections:
You should also handle disconnections by listening to the `disconnect` event on the WalletConnect instance:

```javascript
walletConnect.on('disconnect', (error) => {
  console.error('Disconnected from WalletConnect:', error);
});
```

7. Clean up:
Don't forget to clean up the connection when your server is done using it:

```javascript
walletConnect.on('close', () => {
  console.log('WalletConnect connection closed');
});
```

With these steps, you should be able to send a proposal request to a user's WalletConnect session using a Node.js backend server.
</second_data>

